      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "Analysis of short-term and long-term complications after cemented osteosynthesis for pathological fractures."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Retrospective study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "South Municipal Hospital and the Daniël den Hoed Cancer Centre, Rotterdam, The Netherlands."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SUBJECTS",
          "content": "199 patients consecutive surgically treated between 1978 to 1990 for 233 fractures (161 actual and 72 impending) caused by metastatic lesions of the femur, humerus and tibia."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
          "content": "Local resection of the tumour was followed by endoprostheses (n = 52) and by internal plate osteosynthesis (n = 167); 14 fractures were treated with intramedullary nails. Bone cement was added in 211 cases (91%)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "Pain relief, mobilisation, complications."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Pain relief was achieved in about 90%. 145 (76%) who were treated for fractures of the lower extremity were able to walk again. There were 13 local complications: 26 (11%) implanted devices failed (cumulative probability 40%, after 60 months). In 11 cases the fixation failed after 7 weeks. The failure rate was 16% in the subtrochanteric region treated with an angled blade (probability 70% after four years). The patients' survival rate was 55% after six months and 20% at two years."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Despite the poor life expectancy, our results indicate that hemiarthroplasty or osteosynthesis with bone cement for treatment of pathological (impending) fractures is a safe way to restore limb function and to improve quality of life."
        }
      ]},
